lesson physics-force-in-newtons v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=1 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Force in newtons"
  goal "Read a force in newtons, add forces that act the same way, and work out what is left when forces act against each other."

  look {
    scene 29x15 {
      row kit at=canvas(1, 1) space=2 align=start {
        forces f thing=box push=8 oppose=3
        springscale s max=10 step=2 value=7 unit=N
      }
    }
    say """
      A force is measured in newtons. When two forces pull the same way they add up; when they pull
      against each other, only the difference is left over, and the thing moves the way the bigger
      one is pointing.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.net-force count=2 seed=4
    practice physics.forces-add count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.which-way-n count=2 seed=3
    practice physics.spring-between count=2 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.balance-needed a=4 d=2 easy-a=2 easy-d=1 hard-a=6 hard-d=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.three-forces a=5 c=3 b=6 easy-a=2 easy-c=2 easy-b=3 hard-a=6 hard-c=5 hard-b=9
  }
  remember {
    say "Forces the same way add up. Forces against each other leave the difference."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Tug of war is the whole idea. Two even teams go nowhere, and the rope only moves when one side
    pulls harder, by exactly as much as the difference. The last question has three forces, two one
    way and one the other; the child has to decide which to add before taking anything away.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the two pushes on a box differ plainly, the sums stay under seven, and the three forces are small."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the forces reach nine newtons, the box's two pushes are equal or one apart, and the three forces together pass ten."
  }
}
